#{[17592186052582 :answer/value "\\frac{5|x |}{3 | y|}"] [17592186052585 :answer/value "-\\frac{5|x|}{3|y|}"] [17592186054346 :question/worked-solution "We fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate the error $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$.\n\nFor example, consider the third column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Substituting $x=1.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= -4x + 2 \\\\\n&=-4(1.2) + 2 \\\\\n&=-2.8 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 2x - 5 \\\\\n&= 2(1.2) - 5 \\\\\n&=-2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|-2.8-(-2.6)| \\\\  & =|-0.2| \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the corresponding column:\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ |  | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ |  |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ |  | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ |  | $0.2$ | $1.4$ |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=-4x+2$ | $-1.2$ | $-2$ | $-2.8$ | $-3.6$ | $-4.4$ |\n| $y_2=2x-5$ | $-3.4$ | $-3$ | $-2.6$ | $-2.2$ | $-1.8$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $2.2$ | $1$ | $0.2$ | $1.4$ | $2.6$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.2$, which corresponds to $x=1.2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 1.2\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [17592186054347 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the point of intersection of the two lines.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the solution should lie between $x=13$ and $x=14$. So, we can disregard all values of $x$ that are not between $13$ and $14$.\n\nNow, we evaluate the table entries for $x=13.2$ and $x=13.6$, as follows:\n\n- Substituting $x=13.2$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.2) + 6; = 11.676; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.2) + 3; = 11.58\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.676-11.58| \\\\  & =|0.096| \\\\  & =0.096\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $x=13.6$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{1}|= 0.43x + 6; = 0.43(13.6) + 6; = 11.848; y_{2}|= 0.65x + 3; = 0.65(13.6) + 3; = 11.84\n$$\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|11.848-11.84| \\\\  & =|0.008| \\\\  & =0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the table:\n\n| $x$ | $13.2$ | $13.6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=0.43x+6$ | $11.676$ | $11.848$ |\n| $y_2=0.65x+3$ | $11.58$ | $11.84$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $0.096$ | $0.008$ |\n\nThe smallest error is $0.008$, which corresponds to $x=13.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx 13.6\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [17592186054466 :question/worked-solution "First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspac