van will unload $83.8$ pounds of strawberries at the second store."] [17592186054506 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has real solutions if it has two distinct real solutions ($\\mathcal{D}>0$) or one distinct real solution (\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=0\n$$\n\n). Putting these conditions together, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nTo compute the discriminant, first note the following coefficients: $a=3$, $b=-6k$, and $c=12$. So, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & \\ge 0 \\\\ (-6k)^{2}-4(3)(12) & \\ge 0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2}-144 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2} & \\ge 144 \\\\ {k}^{2} & \\ge 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{4} \\\\ |k| & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mid k \\mid \\geq 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nk \\leq -2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nk \\geq 2\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, then, our solution is\n$k \\in (-\\infty,-2] \\cup [2, \\infty)$."] [17592186054507 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=3k$, $b=12$, and $c=k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (12)^{2}-4(3k)(k) & >0 \\\\ 144-12{k}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 144 & >12{k}^{2} \\\\ 12{k}^{2} & <144 \\\\ {k}^{2} & <12 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |k| & <2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid < 2\\sqrt{3}$, then $-2\\sqrt{3} < k < 2\\sqrt{3}$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $(-2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\nFinally, taking into account that\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have $k\\in(-2\\sqrt{3}, 0) \\cup (0,2\\sqrt{3})$."] [17592186054509 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(kx + 10) &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x &= -5 \\\\\nkx^{2} + 10x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = k, \\qquad b = 10, \\qquad c = 5\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (10)^{2} - 4(k)(5) \\\\\n&= 100 - 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100-20k & >0 \\\\ 100 & >20k \\\\ 20k & <100 \\\\ k & <\\frac{100}{20} \\\\ k & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be positive and an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $k=4$."] [17592186054512 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right].\n$$"] [17592186054512 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\left[(2x-1)^3(x-2)^9\\right]   &= \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)^3\\cdot  \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(2x-1)\\right)^3\\cdot  \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}(x-2)\\right)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= (2\\cdot 3 -1)^3\\cdot (3-2)^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    &= 5^3 \\cdot 1^9\\\\[3pt]\n                                                                                    & = 125.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054513 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)"] [17592186054513 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.\n$$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}{\\left(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)}^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-12}{8}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-3}{2}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054518 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.\n$$"] [17592186054518 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}\n=\\dfrac{\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt{x+8} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}(x-2) & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} & =\\frac{3}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054519 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)"] [17592186054519 :question/worked-solution "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n We also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1) & =(-2)^{2}-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}{\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}\\right)}^{2/3} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054580 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{2\\sin{x}\\cos{2x}}=\n$$"] [17592186054580 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= (\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\cos^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))/(2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (1 - 1)/(\\sqrt{2}\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. The double angle formula for cosine will be useful in this case:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}=\\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}(\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x)/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}(- (\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x))/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  π/4}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sin x}\\right) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054583 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$"] [17592186054584 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{3\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186054584 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= (1 - \\cos (0))/(3\\sin^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{3 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. Again we will use the double angle formula for cosine, but in a different form:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}=1-2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - (1 - 2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(2\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))/(3\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054585 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (-\\pi/2)}\\dfrac{\\cos^2x}{1+\\sin x}=\n$$"] [17592186054585 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= (\\cos^{2} \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))/((1 + \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}((1 + \\sin x)(1 - \\sin x))/(1 + \\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/2)}(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054586 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x-1}{7\\tan^2x}.\n$$"] [17592186054586 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= (\\sec (0) - 1)/(7\\tan^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{7 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity for tangent and secant:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{x}=\\sec^2{x}-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{7\\tan^{2} x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec^{2} x - 1)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(\\sec x - 1)/(7(\\sec x - 1)(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(1)/(7(\\sec x + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(\\sec (0) + 1)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(7(1 + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055053 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  4}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^{3} - 10}{x^{2} - 3x - 2}}$."] [17592186055104 :question/problem "If $y = 0$, then $2|1-{3}^{y}|-|{2}^{y}-4|=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186055114 :question/problem "If $p = 1$, then $|p{|}^{4}-4|p-3{|}^{2}+6=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186055232 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{z \\to  3}((z - 5)^{5})/((z + 3)^{2})$."] [17592186055233 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055234 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a0bb4656cbc3c4c3055635b2f9aa63357ed6422c7e641f3c8982d101cf62c94.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  1/2}(2(f(x))^{4})/((6x - 1)^{3})$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186055235 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c19de6e0431131e0f6dd048e3c1b6544505d0479d98e423944191da690e2ae40.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}\\sqrt{10 - 3f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186055236 :question/problem "![](images/10/10b0662032c021bb027a3ed54de3d03fbc2606f27ea5eaf8c50725dcb14b0854.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\sqrt[3]{6 + 4f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186055297 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  1}\\frac{2\\sqrt{x + 3}}{5x - 1}$."] [17592186055312 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(7 - 7\\cos x)/(8\\sin^{2} \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))$."] [17592186055313 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{4\\cos^{2} x}{1 - \\sin x}$?"] [17592186055314 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos 2x - 1}{10\\sin^{2} x}$."] [17592186055315 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{12\\cos^{2} x}$."] [17592186055316 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/4)}(2 + 2\\cos (4x))/(\\cos^{2} (2x))$."] [17592186055318 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(5\\sec (2x) - 5)/(12\\tan^{2} (2x))$."] [17592186055319 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π)}\\frac{\\cos x + 1}{\\sin^{2} x}$?"] [17592186055320 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/4)}(4 + 4\\csc (2x))/(5\\cot^{2} (2x))$."] [17592186055358 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$ \n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n-1}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)x\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{|x|}{n+1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$  Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that \n$R=\\infty .$"] [17592186055372 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{y \\to  3}(5 - y)^{2}(2y - 5)^{11}$."] [17592186055505 :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are sets. If $\\mid X|= 3$ and $\\mid Y|= 4$, then $|\\mathcal{P}(X)|+|\\mathcal{P}(Y)|=$"] [17592186055938 :tutorial/content "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us about the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are *two distinct real solutions.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is *one distinct real solution.*\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are *no real solutions.*\n\nIf we are given a quadratic equation with an unknown parameter, then we can use the criterion $D > 0$ to determine the values of the unknown parameter such that the quadratic equation has two *distinct* real solutions.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+kx+9=0\n$$\n\nLet's find all the values of $k$ such that this equation has two distinct real solutions.\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$. To compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients of the equation are\n\n$$\na=1,\\quad b=k,\\quad c=9\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (k)^{2}-4(1)(9) & >0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-36 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & >36 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |k| & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nk > 6\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad k < -6\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write our solution as\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,-6) \\cup (6,\\infty)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ inside this interval will give a quadratic equation with *distinct* real solutions."] [17592186055939 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nThe discriminant tells us about the nature of the equation's solutions:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, we have *two distinct real solutions*.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, we have *one repeated real solution* (sometimes called a “double root”).\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, we have *no real solutions.*\n\nCombining the first two conditions, we can say that a quadratic equation *has real solutions* if the discriminant is *non-negative*:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has real solutions, we can use the condition\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D \\geq 0\n$$\n\nto place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x + k = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We want to determine *all* the values of $k$ for which the equation has real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -2, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-2)^{2} - 4(1)(k) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have real solutions, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the inequality\n\n$$\n4 - 4k \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality for the variable $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4k & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4 & \\ge 4k \\\\ 4k & \\le 4 \\\\ k & \\le \\frac{4}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nk\\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty, 1 ]\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with real solutions.\n\nSometimes, these kinds of problems involve solving a simple quadratic inequality. Let's see an example."] [17592186055953 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = x + 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graph corresponding to the system is shown below:\n\n![](images/15/1545b0d9b78f93f18867c20dc40375ba3f3837ea3087a9a9f203cdafb7c2e859.png)\n\nWe can see from the graph that the $x$-value that satisfies the system lies somewhere between $1.0$ and $1.5$.\n\nWe can get a better approximation by setting up a table of values, like the one shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n- First, we calculate the $y$-values on both lines for our different values of $x$.\n- Then, we calculate $\\mid y_2 - y_1 \\mid$ for each value of $x$. We call this the **error.**\n\nFor example, consider the first column of the table, which corresponds to $x=1.0$. Substituting $x=1.0$ into the expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{1} &= x + 1 \\\\\n&= 1.0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2.0 \\\\\ny_{2} &= 4x - 3 \\\\\n&= 4(1.0) - 3 \\\\\n&= 1.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the corresponding error:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}| & =|2.0-1.0| \\\\  & =|1.0| \\\\  & =1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe fill this information into the first column:\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nRepeating this procedure for each column in the table, we arrive at the following result.\n\n| $x$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ | $1.3$ | $1.4$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y_1=x+1$ | $2.0$ | $2.1$ | $2.2$ | $2.3$ | $2.4$ | $2.5$ |\n| $y_2=4x-3$ | $1.0$ | $1.4$ | $1.8$ | $2.2$ | $2.6$ | $3.0$ |\n| $\\mid y_1-y_2 \\mid$ | $1.0$ | $0.7$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFinally, we look for the smallest error. In the bottom row of the table, we see that $0.1$ is the smallest error. This corresponds to $x=1.3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx\\approx1.3\n$$\n\nis our approximate solution."] [17592186055954 :tutorial/content "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={(\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055955 :tutorial/content "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055956 :tutorial/content "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,\n$$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={(\\lim_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186055961 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{2\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we attempt to evaluate this limit by directly substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}\\frac{\\sin 2x}{2\\cos x} &= (\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (π))/(2\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before attempting to evaluate the limit, we obtain a more clear result.\n\nTo simplify the fraction in this particular case, the double angle formula for sine will be useful:\n\n$$\n\\sin{2x}=2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sin   2 x}{2 \\cos   x}} & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cos   x}{2 \\cos   x}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 \\sin   x \\cancel{\\cos   x}}{2 \\cancel{\\cos   x}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\lim_{x \\to  \\pi  / 2} \\sin   x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\sin   ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIn other cases, we may need to use a different trigonometric identity."] [17592186056063 :tutorial/content "We can use the same method to multiply decimals even when they are greater than $1$. For example, to compute\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8\n$$\n\nwe first ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{array}{cccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{aligned} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}}\\end{aligned}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\times \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{red}{0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $4.7$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.8$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $376$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large{ {2} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8 = 3.76 \\,\n$$"] [17592186056065 :tutorial/content "Decimal subtraction works in a similar way to whole number subtraction.\n\nAs an example, let's find the value of $0.8 - 0.5$.\n\nFirst, we write one number above the other, lining up the decimal points and placing a decimal point below the others in our answer:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 5;\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n0.8 - 0.5= 0.3\n$$\n\nThis is just like $8-5=3$, but with the decimal point in a different position."] [17592186056069 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+12 & =13 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2+6 & =8 & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 3+4 & =7 & {\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + \\underbrace{3x + 4x}_{7x} + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓\n$$"] [17592186059533 :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $4$th quadrant. Using basic trigonometry, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{x} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}(-{31}^{∘}) \\\\  & \\approx 12\\cdot 0.857 \\\\  & \\approx 10.3 \\\\ {a}_{y} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}(-{31}^{∘}) \\\\  & \\approx 12\\cdot (-0.515) \\\\  & \\approx -6.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle 10.3, -6.2 \\rangle \n\t\t= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}10.3 \\\\ - 6.2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [17592186059534 :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{x} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =60\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{y} & =|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =60\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059539 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =45\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{77}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 45\\cdot 12\\cdot 0.224\\hspace{0.167em}951 \\\\  & \\approx 121.474\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059540 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186059542 :question/worked-solution "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{17}\\cdot 146\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{17}\\cdot 146\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics."] [17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\underbrace{\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine."] [17592186060912 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a560689c3872d945fdbea036ad5963a4c78f075c33637e116da9b04d49afc0.png)\n\nThe angle ${\\color{blue}\\theta}$ is the angle that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the **positive $x$-axis**.\n\nNow, from $\\triangle OXA$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{|XA|}{|OX|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{y}}{{a}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060914 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes the angle $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {a}_{x}=|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & {a}_{y}=|\\mathbf{a}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle = \\langle |\\mathbf{a}| \\cos\\theta, |\\mathbf{a}| \\sin\\theta \\rangle .\n$$"] [17592186060917 :tutorial/content "The **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nFor example, suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now define the **dot product** (or **scalar product**) of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060919 :tutorial/content "Two nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are **perpendicular** if the angle between them is\n\n$$\n\\theta=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe can denote that two vectors are perpendicular using the symbol $\\perp$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn general, the dot product of two perpendicular vectors is $0$. If $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, and\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe converse is also true. If $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are nonzero vectors, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$"] [17592186061199 :answer/value "\\frac{|p |}{9 | q|}"] [17592186062901 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186062902 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186062904 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x) \\ne f(3)"] [17592186063386 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{-}}f(x) \\ne lim_(x → - 1^{+})f(x)"] [17592186063387 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) \\ne f(-1)"] [17592186063389 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  - 1^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186063911 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x}.\n$$"] [17592186063911 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sec x -1}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sec 0-1}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both the numerator and denominator by $\\cos x$, we can rewrite the expression and evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{\\sec x - 1}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}((\\sec x - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}((\\frac{1}{\\cos x} - 1)\\cos x)/(x\\cos x) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos x}{x} \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot (1)/(\\cos (0)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063916 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1-x & x>1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)= f(1)\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$"] [17592186063916 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-x) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1ac7fdbbedfa59db5b49a3554cf1fb62b6d1fcafb346536f76599acdd82dd63.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186063917 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}3x+2, & x<2 \\\\ 6, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x>2\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186063917 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2) = 6,$ so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(3x+2) \\\\  & =3(2)+2 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$ $\\ \\\\[1ex]$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=  8,\n$$ while $f(2) = 6.$ ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2).$"] [17592186063933 :question/worked-solution "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = \\lim_{h \\to  0}(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= \\lim_{h \\to  0}((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{h \\to  0}(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063934 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= \\lim_{x \\to  2}(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}\\left(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to  2}(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$."] [17592186063994 :question/worked-solution "First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hsp