m}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1,289.71$."] [17592186064033 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to 2}(\\sqrt{bx+3})=\\sqrt{2b+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\ \\sqrt{2b+3} & =5 \\\\ 2b+3 & =25 \\\\ 2b & =22 \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064034 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $h(x),$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}{log}_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity."] [17592186064118 :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}({x}^{2}+2kx+1) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2k(1)+1 \\\\  & =2+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 2+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity."] [17592186064142 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x}.\n$$"] [17592186064142 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos{3x}}{x} = \\dfrac{1-\\cos(3\\cdot 0)}{0} = \\dfrac 00\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos \\theta}{\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{x} &= \\lim_{x \\to  0}(3 \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3 \\cdot x}) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot lim_(x → 0)\\frac{1 - \\cos 3x}{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta=3x$. Then, since $\\theta \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}3x}}{\\color{blue}3x} = \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos {\\color{blue}\\theta}}{\\color{blue}\\theta} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\cdot\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos{\\color{black}3x}}{\\color{black}3x}= 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"] [17592186064149 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\cos^2 y-1}{y}.\n$$"] [17592186064149 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $y \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} = (\\cos^{2} (0) - 1)/(0) = \\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{y \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-\\cos y}{y} = 0\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos^{2} y - 1}{y} &= \\lim_{y \\to  0}((\\cos y - 1)(\\cos y + 1))/(y) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{\\cos y - 1}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{1 - \\cos y}{y} \\cdot lim_(y → 0)(\\cos y + 1) \\\\\n&=-0 \\cdot (\\cos 0 + 1) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064512 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}2x-1, & x<1 \\\\ 2-x, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186064512 :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$” is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ is true. Indeed, we have that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$” is true. Since all statements “$f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$”; $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$; $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [17592186064513 :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 x + 3 , & x < - 2 \\\\ - 1 , & x = - 2 \\\\ - {x}^{2} + 1 , & x > - 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x =-2$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-2)}f(x)$ exists\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-2)}f(x) = f(-2)$\n4. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x =-2$"] [17592186064753 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186064754 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 3$ and $f(3) = 1$, then what is $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}\\sqrt{x^{2} - f(x)}$?"] [17592186064789 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}(1 - \\cos^{2} x)/(x(1 + \\cos x))$."]}
